{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '440,620p' test/yang/clojure_collection_order_test.cljc && sed -n '96,125p' /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1795017757000-architect-cc4-dart-map-order-ruling.gpt-6-astra.findings.md\"","aggregated_output":"                    :let [ctors (collection-applications (yang/compile form))]]\n              (is (= 1 (count ctors)))\n              (is (every? #(ascending? (element-keys %)) ctors))))))))\n  (testing \"recorded corpus\"\n    (doseq [[label elements] set-corpus]\n      (testing (str label)\n        (let [variants (into []\n                             (mapcat (fn [p]\n                                       (for [rep [:vec-set :hash-set]]\n                                         (build-set rep (permute elements p)))))\n                             (all-permutations (count elements)))\n              snaps (mapv compile-snapshot variants)]\n          (is (apply = snaps))\n          (doseq [form variants\n                  :let [ctors (collection-applications (yang/compile form))]]\n            (is (every? #(ascending? (element-keys %)) ctors)))))))\n  (testing \"sizes 8/9/16/32: recorded permutations\"\n    (doseq [n map-corpus-sizes\n            :let [elements (sized-set-elements n)]]\n      (testing (str \"size \" n)\n        (let [variants (into []\n                             (mapcat (fn [p]\n                                       (for [rep [:vec-set :hash-set]]\n                                         (build-set rep (permute elements p)))))\n                             (recorded-permutations n 1795017757001))\n              snaps (mapv compile-snapshot variants)]\n          (is (apply = snaps))\n          (doseq [form variants\n                  :let [ctors (collection-applications (yang/compile form))]]\n            (is (every? #(ascending? (element-keys %)) ctors)))))))\n  (testing \"compiled element occurrences survive: expressions that evaluate\n            to one value keep their compiled occurrences\"\n    (let [form #{(list '- 'n 1) (list '- 1 'n)}\n          ctor (first (collection-applications (yang/compile form)))]\n      (is (= 3 (count (:operands ctor))))\n      (is (= 2 (count (element-keys ctor))))))\n  (testing \"the constant fast path stays a literal\"\n    (is (= {:type :literal :value #{1 2 3}}\n           (yang/compile #{1 2 3})))))\n\n\n(defn- root-of\n  [ast]\n  (:root (vm/ast->semantic-bytecode ast)))\n\n(defn- independent-pair-keys\n  \"The expected pair keys of a source map `form`, built independently of the\n   emitter: each entry's key and value compile standalone (empty\n   environment, as the emitter's children do) and project to their roots.\"\n  [form]\n  ;; children compile as the emitter compiles them: tail? false, empty env\n  (->> (map (fn [k v]\n              (let [ck (yang/compile-form k false {})\n                    cv (yang/compile-form v false {})]\n                (vec [(root-of ck) (root-of cv)])))\n            (keys form) (vals form))\n       (sort jing/canonical-compare)\n       vec))\n\n(deftest order-pairing-multiplicity-oracle-test\n  (testing \"pairs stay intact, the seed stays first, the pair vector sorts\"\n    (doseq [form [{:a (list '+ 'n 1), :b (list '+ 'n 2)}\n                  {(list 'first 'xs) :v, :k [1 (list '+ 'n 3)]}\n                  {:x #{(list '+ 'n 4)}, :y {:in (list '+ 'n 5)}}]\n            ;; the outermost constructor: the walk collects innermost first\n            :let [ctor (last (map-constructors (yang/compile form)))\n                  roots (child-roots ctor)\n                  pairs (pair-keys ctor)]]\n      (is (= empty-map-seed-root (first roots)))\n      (is (odd? (count roots)))\n      (is (= (+ 1 (* 2 (count form))) (count roots)))\n      (is (ascending? pairs))\n      (is (= (independent-pair-keys form)\n             (vec (sort jing/canonical-compare pairs)))\n          \"the emitted pairs are exactly the independently compiled source\n           pairs, none lost or mixed\")))\n  (testing \"the value root breaks equal key roots\"\n    ;; two NaN keys are never = to each other (both survive the host map)\n    ;; and the codec makes every NaN one quiet NaN, so both compile to one\n    ;; key code: only the value root can order these pairs\n    (let [form (assoc {} ##NaN :left ##NaN (list '+ 'n 1))\n          ctor (last (map-constructors (yang/compile form)))\n          pairs (pair-keys ctor)]\n      (is (= 2 (count pairs)) (str \"both NaN entries survive: \" (count pairs)))\n      (is (ascending? pairs))\n      (is (= (first (first pairs)) (first (second pairs))))\n      (is (= (mapv second pairs)\n             (vec (sort jing/canonical-compare (mapv second pairs)))))))\n  (testing \"identical compiled pairs tie without occurrence loss\"\n    (let [form (assoc {} ##NaN (list '+ 'n 0) ##NaN (list '+ 'n 0))\n          ctor (last (map-constructors (yang/compile form)))\n          pairs (pair-keys ctor)]\n      (is (= 2 (count pairs)))\n      (is (= (first pairs) (second pairs)))\n      (is (= 5 (count (:operands ctor))) \"seed plus both pair occurrences\")))\n  (testing \"identical compiled set elements tie without occurrence loss\"\n    ;; A whole-element tie needs two host-unequal elements with one\n    ;; compiled content: two NaNs are the only such pair, and every host\n    ;; set constructor available to the form API (reader, conj, into,\n    ;; hash-set) collapses them, so the tie is unconstructable at the\n    ;; emitter's input boundary -- recorded as the boundary, never\n    ;; silently skipped. What IS reachable is occurrence preservation of\n    ;; equal compiled subtrees inside one set:\n    (let [inner (list '+ 'n 0)\n          form #{inner (list '+ 'n 1)}\n          ctor (first (collection-applications (yang/compile form)))\n          keys (element-keys ctor)]\n      (is (= 2 (count keys)))\n      (is (ascending? keys))\n      (is (= 3 (count (:operands ctor))))))\n  (testing \"source-position and node metadata never choose the order\"\n    (let [plain {:a (list '+ 'n 1), :b (list '+ 'n 2)}\n          positioned (with-meta plain {:line 3 :column 4 :end-line 3 :end-column 9})\n          noted (with-meta plain {:note :source-form})\n          value-meta {(with-meta (list '+ 'n 1) {:line 8}) :a\n                      (list '+ 'n 2) :b}\n          snap (fn [f] (compile-snapshot f))\n          ctor-roots (fn [f] (child-roots (first (map-constructors (yang/compile f)))))]\n      (is (= (snap plain) (snap positioned)))\n      (is (= (snap plain) (snap noted)))\n      (is (= (ctor-roots plain) (ctor-roots positioned)))\n      (is (= (ctor-roots plain) (ctor-roots noted)))\n      (is (ascending? (pair-keys (first (map-constructors (yang/compile value-meta))))))))\n  (testing \"semantic metadata in literal payloads stays content\"\n    (let [with-sem {(list 'quote (with-meta [1 2] {:sem true})) (list '+ 'n 1)\n                    :plain 2}\n          without {:plain 2}]\n      (is (not= (root-of (yang/compile with-sem))\n                (root-of (yang/compile without))))\n      (is (ascending? (pair-keys (first (map-constructors (yang/compile with-sem)))))))))\n\n\n;; The outcome lane the arity payload check runs on: the same light\n;; convention clojure_test runs (default registry + stream/cell/data\n;; modules + the portable adapter), portable across the four hosts.\n(def ^:private outcome-modules\n  (let [host (fn [name entries]\n               (fn [registry]\n                 (module/register-host-module\n                   registry name\n                   (into {} (map (fn [[op f _]] [op f])) entries)\n                   (into {} (map (fn [[op _ p]] [op p])) entries))))\n        portable [['nth\n                   (yang/outcome-adapter data/refusal-message\n                                         (get data/data-module 'nth))\n                   (get data/data-profiles 'nth)]]]\n    (-> (module/default-registry)\n        module/register-stream-module\n        module/register-cell-module\n        (data/register-data-module {::data/max-items 1048576})\n        ((host 'portable portable)))))\n\n(def ^:private outcome-opts\n  {:convention :arg-vector\n   :outcomes true\n   :native (set (keys vm/primitives))\n   :adapters {'data/nth 'portable/nth}})\n\n(defn- run-outcome\n  \"The raw outcome the last form of `forms` returns on the walker outcome\n   lane.\"\n  [forms]\n  (-> (tu/make-observer-session (tu/create-vm {:modules outcome-modules}))\n      (tu/queue-ast! (yang/compile-program forms true outcome-opts))\n      tu/run-session\n      :consumer\n      vm/value))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; RULING 3 test 1: the reproduced arity-map regression\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(def ^:private cohort-forms\n  \"The cc4 cohort member source (r2 defect evidence): the destructuring,\n   floats and repeated subtrees of the reproduced divergence, compiled with\n   its original options and supplied coordinates.\"\n  '[(defn replay\n      [x]\n      (let [[a b] [x 1.5]]\n        (+ (identity ^:replay a) (identity ^:replay a) b)))\nclojure -M:test -n yang.clojure-callsite-test\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile test --config-merge '{:ns-regexp \"^yang\\\\.clojure-callsite-test$\"}'\nbb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj --only yang.clojure-callsite-test\nbb src/dev/jolt_j2_lane.clj core --only '^yang.clojure-callsite-test$'\n```\n\nRun the new portable namespace through the corresponding four selectors as well; run affected compiler/manifest tests and kondo on touched files. Record exact commands, toolchains, source/test pins, executed counts, and refusal inventories. r2's 45 JVM / 40 Node-Dart test counts are historical baselines, not counts to force after adding tests. J2's four-namespace manifest refusal MUST be resolved by its owner before four-host acceptance; zero Jolt children is not a pass. Observe the repository's targeted-test/remote-lane rules and required `dao.stream` merge gate; do not launch a branch full suite as part of this ruling.\n\n**Re-pinning is REQUIRED.** This repair changes canonical executable bytes. After reviewing the new operand vectors and establishing raw four-host agreement, record one post-fix reference bundle and use that identical bundle as fixed expectations on every host. Re-record the complete cohort, including unchanged members, so root, full C1/key digests, per-format provenance, producing logs, publication/table/record/manifest addresses, and compiler revision pins remain mutually consistent. No Dart-specific expectation, no runtime recomputation of expected digests, and no origin/path/name normalization are permitted.\n\nKeep the r2 divergence file, raw logs, and findings immutable as historical defect evidence. Archive the original active snapshots/captures under an explicitly pre-fix name before replacing any active fixture; publish a before/after ledger keyed by compiler revision and input pins. Re-pinning a failing host in isolation is not a repair. A JVM address changing too is expected and is not grounds to preserve the old broken ordering.\n\nThe fix's regression result does not itself close cc4 adoption. Resume cc4's declared B/S, pre/post-port and cross-check-host budget work only after determinism gates pass. Preserve the existing finite ceiling declaration; record any exceedance honestly. Pin this ordering fix separately from Phase 2 `d60038ef`, so its additional compile-time content projection/sorting costs and changed artifact sizes are visible confounders rather than falsely attributed to the continuation port. The r2 report states those measurements were not executed. D1 remains dissolved under the dispatch; this ruling does not revive it.\n\n## RULING 4 — versioning, addresses, and legacy artifacts\n\n**Yes: there is a one-time canonical shift for affected newly compiled artifacts under this new compiler revision.** Changed collection applications change ancestor roots and may change occurrence paths, C1 keys/tables, instruction vectors/provenance, macro input/output records, and containing publication addresses. Even an application elsewhere in a changed root gets a different C1 key because the root is part of that key. There is no requirement that every artifact changes.\n\n**MUST:** advance the Clojure frontend manifest revision from the inspected `\"c1-1\"` to `\"c1-2\"` and declare its lowering profile as `:yang.clojure/constructed-collections-v1` (if the dispatch base has already advanced, choose and record a fresh nonconflicting successor before recording expectations). Keep the C1 marker value `1` and the existing C1 support claim: identity/coverage schema has not changed. Keep SPI and UAST/execution grammar versions unchanged. The shared UAST-to-instruction lowerer has not changed for a given UAST, so its own lowering-profile stamp must not be bumped merely because this frontend now supplies different input trees. Pin the compiler commit and this frontend ordering profile with the replay fixtures.\n\nThis follows the original design's §3.1 distinction between frontend/lowering revisions and execution grammar revisions, and `docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md` §5.2.1–5.2.2: content integrity, derivation verification under an exact profile, and executable loadability are distinct obligations. The existing manifest function is at `src/cljc/yin/repl/frontends.cljc:43–54`.\n\n**Legacy artifacts MUST continue to load at their original addresses**, subject to their already-existing content/contract/dependency checks. Loaders must not sort their operands, rehash them under the new policy, rewrite stored facts, or relabel old C1 keys as new ones. Old executable operand order remains old executable behavior. No storage rewrite or destructive migration is required. Recompilation/republishing creates new immutable artifacts and new addresses; consumers electing to upgrade update their references explicitly. Old side tables remain attached to old roots, and absent legacy provenance is not fabricated.\n\n**Address guarantee:** identical canonical bytes retain identical content addresses regardless of compiler version. Identical source text does NOT promise identical compiled addresses across unpinned compiler/profile revisions. C1 §5.2 replay equality already pins the compiler/profile revision and all other listed inputs; it never guaranteed source-to-address stability across compiler upgrades. Reproducing an old compilation requires its old pinned compiler/profile (or loading its retained output), not silently applying this new normalization. Historical evidence remains valid evidence of its own artifacts, not a certificate for newly compiled roots.\n\nAcceptance remains pending the new fix, independently checked re-pins, four-host regression results, legacy execution evidence, and the separate cc4 budget closure. This document rules policy and bounds; it does not claim those gates have passed.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
